## 00 — THE VOID

The purpose
Before the visitor understands Mael, they need to feel the scale of his world.
There is no explanation.
No headline.
No navigation competing for attention.
No "AI-powered platform."
No CTA.
Just space.
The first question we want to create is:
What is that? What is this?

## 00.1 — Initial frame

The page loads into almost complete darkness.
Not a black background with stars sprinkled across it.
Darkness with depth.
There should be subtle differences in black—enough that the visitor eventually realizes they're looking into something enormous.
Very sparse stars.
A few distant points.
Some almost imperceptible cosmic dust.
Nothing moves quickly enough to immediately register as an animation.
Important:
There should be no Mael yet.
Or, more accurately:
the visitor shouldn't be able to see him yet.

## 00.2 — The first movement

After a short period, something changes.
Not Mael.
The environment.
A tiny particle moves across the frame.
Another responds.
A faint distortion passes through the darkness.
Almost like gravity.
The visitor may not consciously notice it.
But the environment suddenly feels alive.
This is important because our Visual Bible says Mael doesn't "snap into existence." Energy is continuous, particles respond to movement, and nothing should feel like a conventional UI animation.

## 00.3 — Mael reveals himself

Very slowly, a silhouette becomes visible in the distance.
At first:
Is that a person?
Then:
Yes.
But barely.
He's enormous relative to the environment.
He's standing far away.
Facing away from us.
We don't see his face.
We don't know what he is.
Inside his silhouette:
stars, and cosmic dust.
Not a galaxy pasted onto him.
Rather, his form seems to be made from the same environment we're looking into.
Some dust passes through him.
Some light seems to exist within him.

## 00.4 — The Horizon

This is where we introduce the visual identifier.
Very faintly:
the Horizon.
Not gold yet.
Not glowing.
It might initially appear almost white/amber because it's so dim.
It's slightly elliptical.
Irregular.
Different thickness around its circumference.
One side catches more light.
The surrounding particles subtly bend around it.
The visitor might initially interpret it as:
halo?
But it shouldn't quite behave like one.
Because it's not a halo.
It's the gravitational field around Mael.
Our bible explicitly establishes that the Horizon replaces conventional facial features and is how the viewer recognizes Mael.

## 00.5 — No words

I actually want to be very strict here.
No text.
Not even:
MAEL
Not yet.
Because the visitor hasn't met him.
We're allowing the visual language to introduce him first.

## 00.6 — The scroll

This is where the website becomes interesting.
The visitor scrolls.
Instead of the page simply moving downward:
the scroll becomes the camera's timeline.
The viewport remains almost fixed.
The world changes.
Mael begins to move.
That carries us directly into:

## 01 — THE PRESENCE

The transition shouldn't feel like:
Section 00 ended → Section 01 started.
It should feel like:
Something noticed you.

