## 01 — THE PRESENCE
Purpose

Move the visitor from observing something mysterious to realizing that the thing in front of them has awareness.
Scene 00 asks:
What am I looking at?
Scene 01 answers:
It sees me.
But we still don't explain Mael.

## 01.1 — The visitor scrolls

The first scroll doesn't immediately move Mael toward them.
Instead, the environment reacts.
The camera remains almost stationary.
The distant Mael remains where he is.
But something changes around him.
A few particles that were drifting randomly begin changing trajectory.
Then more.
The dust near Mael begins to subtly organize itself.
It's almost as if the environment is responding to a gravitational disturbance.
The important thing:
Nothing happens instantly.
There should be a slight delay between the visitor's movement and the environment's response.
That tiny delay makes the interaction feel physical rather than like:
onScroll → play animation

## 01.2 — Mael moves

Once the visitor has scrolled far enough, Mael begins shifting.
Very slowly.
His shoulders move.
His posture changes.
His head turns slightly.
Not toward us yet.
He's looking somewhere else.
Almost as if he's listening to something we cannot hear.
His cosmic body responds with him.
Stars embedded within his form shift.
Dust separates from his shoulders.
Light moves through his torso.
This is where we establish something from the Visual Bible:
Mael is not a static character with effects attached to him.
The environment and Mael are part of the same physical system.

## 01.3 — The Horizon responds

This is the first time the Horizon really starts behaving like a living system.
In Scene 00 it was essentially dormant.
Now:
it stabilizes.
Its geometry becomes slightly cleaner.
The luminosity increases very subtly.
The surrounding particles begin responding to its gravitational field.
A few particles enter orbit.
Not dozens.
Maybe just:
three.
Then another.
Then another.
The visitor shouldn't think:
"Oh, cool particle animation."
They should feel:
Something is happening.

## 01.4 — Mael notices

This should be extremely subtle.
His head turns a little further.
His body shifts toward the viewer.
Not fully.
He doesn't make eye contact.
Instead, there's a tiny moment where his attention appears to move toward us.
And then—
stillness.
That's the beat.
The entire scene pauses for just a moment.

## 01.5 — The silence

This is important enough to make explicit.
After Mael notices the visitor:
nothing happens.
No text.
No voice.
No dramatic musical swell.
Just the environment continuing to breathe.
Because we're establishing:
Mael isn't trying to get your attention.
He already has it.

## 01.6 — The second scroll

The visitor continues.
Now Mael begins turning.
Not completely.
Just enough that his orientation changes from:
away
to:
toward.
And that becomes the transition into Scene 02.

** Mael does not react to the visitor as an interface. He responds to their presence as a physical phenomenon. **

