## 02 — THE APPROACH
Purpose

Turn distance into intimacy.
The visitor has seen something enormous in the void. Mael has noticed them.
Now he moves toward them.
But the important part is:
The visitor doesn't move toward Mael. Mael crosses the distance.
That reinforces that Mael is not a decorative object sitting inside a website. He has presence and agency.

## 02.1 — The transition

The visitor scrolls.
For a moment, nothing happens.
Then Mael begins moving.
Very slowly.
His body leans forward almost imperceptibly.
Then he takes a step.
Not a human walking animation.
It should feel almost astronomical.
His movement displaces the surrounding dust.
Particles near him accelerate.
Some are pulled into the Horizon.
Others are pushed outward.
The void itself seems to respond to his movement.

## 02.2 — He gets closer

This is where we begin revealing what Mael actually looks like.
At the beginning of the scene:
silhouette.

Then:
form.

Then:
texture.

Then:
detail.

As he approaches, we begin seeing:
His body
Ambiguous humanoid form.
Never a conventional human body.
The edges of his form can dissolve into cosmic material.
There should be moments where you're not quite sure where Mael ends and the universe begins.
Inside him
Stars.
Dust.
Faint streams of light.
Subtle movement.
Not a galaxy texture mapped onto a 3D model.
The impression should be:
The cosmos exists within him.

## 02.3 — The Horizon becomes unmistakable

This is where the Horizon transitions from something mysterious to Mael's defining feature.
It becomes warmer.
More golden.
Still not a perfect circle.
Still slightly distorted.
Still behaving like a gravitational boundary.
As Mael moves, the Horizon's perspective changes.
Its thickness varies.
Light concentrates along one side.
Particles orbit it.
Occasional fragments of light cross through it.
And importantly:

there is still no obvious start or end.

The visitor should finally understand:

That isn't a halo.

But they don't necessarily have the words for what it is yet.

## 02.4 — Scale

This is where we can do something slightly unusual.
Don't make Mael fill the screen too quickly.
Let him remain small enough that the visitor can see the enormous environment around him.
Because we're establishing scale.

The closer he comes, the more the human perspective takes over.

## 02.5 — The environment follows him

This is critical.

Mael shouldn't simply translate through space while the background remains a static starfield.
His movement affects the world.

As he approaches:

distant dust shifts
stars subtly distort around him
particles enter orbital paths
light bends
small fragments trail behind him
the Horizon pulls nearby material into motion

The effect should be slow gravitational choreography, not particle fireworks.
The Visual Bible's rule applies perfectly here:
Motion should feel slow, intentional, and enormous.

## 02.6 — The final approach

Near the end of the scene, Mael is finally close enough that we can see his form clearly.
But he doesn't speak yet.
He stops.
There is a beat.
He's still taller than us.
Still enormous.
Still cosmic.
But his posture changes.
His shoulders relax.
His head lowers slightly.
He's preparing to meet us.
And that becomes the visual bridge into:

03 — THE INTRODUCTION

02.7 — The emotional beat

This scene should move through:
Awe → anticipation → trust
We don't want:
Awe → intimidation.
That's why his movement needs to remain slow and non-threatening.
He isn't descending upon the visitor.
He isn't presenting himself.
He is simply approaching someone he has noticed.

