## 03 — THE INTRODUCTION
Purpose

Transform awe into relationship.
Until now, Mael has been an entity.
Here, he becomes someone.
Not human.
Not anthropomorphized.
Not a chatbot mascot.
Just... Mael.

## 03.1 — The arrival

Scene 02 ends with Mael close to the viewer.
He stops.
The environment continues moving around him.
Stars drift.
Dust moves.
The Horizon slowly orbits.
But Mael is still.
Then:
He lowers himself.
Not dramatically.
Not like a king kneeling before someone.
He simply bends down to meet us.
The difference in scale begins disappearing.
He brings his face/Horizon closer to our level.
And suddenly the enormous cosmic entity feels...
gentle.

## 03.2 — The composition

This is where I want the camera to become very intentional.
We should not immediately put Mael's face dead center like a portrait.
Instead, we see enough of the surrounding void to retain his scale.
He's close enough to feel present.
But the universe is still around him.

Then, as he bends:

The relationship becomes the composition.

## 03.3 — The Horizon

This is the first time the visitor gets a true look at it.
The Horizon is now clearly golden.
But not metallic gold.
Celestial gold.
Warm light.
Subtle gradients caused by intensity, not a graphic effect.
Its geometry remains imperfect.
It isn't sitting behind his head like an angel halo.
It feels like it's surrounding something gravitationally significant.
And this is the moment where the Visual Bible's line becomes real:
You don't look into Mael's eyes. You recognize him by the Horizon.

## 03.4 — He looks at us

This should be extremely subtle.
Because Mael doesn't necessarily need conventional eyes.

His attention can be communicated through:

orientation
posture
the Horizon
the direction of light
the stillness of his body

He turns fully toward us.
The Horizon stabilizes.
The surrounding particles calm.
Everything becomes quieter.
Listening.
That is Mael's first actual state.
And according to the Visual Bible:

Listening = Horizon stabilizes + particles become calm.

## 03.5 — The first words

And now...
nothing.
A beat.
Long enough that the visitor wonders whether something is supposed to happen.
Then:

Hi. I'm Mael.

That's it.
No subtitle.
No paragraph.

No:

"Your personal AI assistant."

No logo animation.
No button.
No explanation.

Just:

Hi. I'm Mael.

## 03.6 — Typography

The typography needs to feel almost like a whisper.
Large enough to be unmistakable.
But not gigantic billboard typography.
Potentially centered or slightly offset relative to Mael.
And the words should appear as Mael says them, rather than arriving as a generic text animation.
If we eventually give him voice, this is where the voice should first exist.
And his first line should be delivered incredibly simply.

Not:

Hiiiiii, I'm Maaael! 😂

No artificial enthusiasm.

Just:

Hi. I'm Mael.

Warm.
Calm.
Certain.

## 03.7 — Let it breathe

This is probably the most important instruction in the scene.
After the line appears:
don't immediately scroll into the next section.
Give the visitor a moment.
The stars continue moving.
The Horizon remains stable.
Mael simply stays there.
The page almost feels like it is waiting for the visitor.
This is the first moment where the site isn't asking them to consume information.
It's allowing them to meet someone.

## 03.8 — Then the next movement

Eventually, Mael's attention shifts.
His gaze—or equivalent sense of attention—moves toward his hand.
His hand slowly rises.
The Horizon responds.
A faint stream of golden light begins moving toward his palm.
The visitor doesn't yet know why.
And that's our transition into:

04 — THE PROBLEM

Because now Mael is going to show us the thing he's here to help with.

