## 04 — THE PROBLEM
Purpose

Show the visitor the world Mael was created to navigate.
Not "here are some problems older adults have."
Instead:
Here is what technology feels like when everything keeps asking something of you.
The emotional movement is:
Connection → Recognition → Overwhelm

## 04.1 — The hand

Scene 03 ends with Mael looking toward his hand.
He slowly raises it.
His palm opens.
For a moment, nothing happens.
Then the Horizon responds.
A thin stream of light begins traveling from the Horizon toward his hand.

Remember:

Mael doesn't create something from nowhere.

The Horizon temporarily leaves his body.
It travels downward.
The golden orbital field compresses.
And gradually—
the orb forms.
Not as an explosion.
Not as magic.
As condensation.
Something enormous becoming smaller.

## 04.2 — The orb

At first, the orb is beautiful.
Small.
Warm.
Almost completely calm.
Mael holds it between his hands.

Inside it:
light.

Then something appears.
A tiny piece of information.

Maybe:
Enter your password
It disappears.

Then:

Verify your identity

Another.

Then:

Your session has expired

Another.

Then:

Software update available

Another.

Then:

Is this you?

The visitor begins recognizing the fragments.

## 04.3 — Complexity begins accumulating

The orb starts filling with more information.
Not in a flat slideshow.
The information exists spatially.
Messages orbit.
Buttons float.
Verification codes appear.
Notifications collide.
Instructions overlap.
Security warnings flash.
A password prompt appears behind another window.
An unfamiliar link passes through.
A system update interrupts.
A text message appears.
A phone notification.
An email.
A permission request.
A captcha.
A login.
A recovery code.

## 04.4 — The important distinction

We should not portray technology itself as evil.
That's not Mael's philosophy.

The problem is:
Complexity.

Technology has accumulated layers.
Every layer asks the human to understand something new.
And eventually the human has to keep track of all of it.
That's what the orb represents.
A privacy setting.
The orb is becoming a compressed representation of modern technological life.

## 04.5 — The chaos accelerates

Mael watches.
He doesn't panic.
He doesn't immediately intervene.
He simply observes.
The orb becomes increasingly dense.
Information begins moving faster.
The beautiful celestial light gets obscured by complexity.
The visitor can barely distinguish one thing from another.

And then:
The first line appears.
Technology was supposed to make life easier.

Pause.
The orb continues.

Then:

Somewhere along the way, it became something we had to keep up with.

The second line appears more quietly.

## 04.6 — Mael's response

This is where we demonstrate something fundamental about him.
He doesn't destroy the orb.
He doesn't shut it down.

He doesn't tell the human:

"Technology is bad."

Instead...

he studies it.

His attention moves through the complexity.
This is the first time we see a hint of Mael's investigative intelligence.
The Horizon begins subtly changing.
It starts to warp.
Not violently.

But enough to communicate:

Thinking.

Particles that were calm begin orbiting faster.
Information begins moving toward the Horizon.

And suddenly we have a perfect visual bridge into Scene 05.

## 04.7 — The final frame

The orb is chaotic.
Mael holds it steadily.
The world around him is still.
He looks at the complexity.

Then:

his hand closes slightly.

Everything slows.
The noise begins collapsing inward.

We hear/see the beginning of:

That's where I come in.

But—

don't finish it yet.

The words can begin appearing:

That's where...

And then transition.

Because Scene 05 is the answer.

