## 08 — THE PRODUCT
Purpose

Answer the question:

"Okay. I understand Mael. What can Mael actually do for me?"
This is where we move from emotion to comprehension.
But we still don't want a conventional SaaS feature grid.
The product should feel like the human application of everything we just witnessed.

## 08.1 — The transition from the film

We ended Scene 07 with the cosmic particles returning to the stars.
The screen doesn't hard-cut to a dashboard.
Instead, one golden particle remains.
It moves across the screen.
Then another.
Then another.

They begin forming something familiar:

the Mael interface.

The cosmic language gradually becomes product language.

The visitor realizes:

Oh. This is Mael.

## 08.2 — The product introduction

We need a simple statement.

Meet Mael.

Then:

A calmer way to navigate technology.

That's the bridge.
We've established why.
Now we show how.

## 08.3 — Don't show the whole application at once

This is important.

I don't want a giant screenshot of the entire UI with 14 buttons.
Instead, we demonstrate one human problem at a time.
The interface should feel almost like we're watching Mael work.

## 08.4 — UNDERSTAND
Scenario

A user encounters something confusing.

Maybe:

"Your account requires two-factor authentication."

The user asks:

"What does that mean?"

Mael responds in plain language.

Something like:

"It means the website wants to make sure it's really you. You'll use your password, then confirm with a code sent to your phone."

Then:

"I'll walk you through it."

The visual interface simplifies.

What we're demonstrating:

Mael translates technology into human language.

## 08.5 — REMEMBER

Then the interface transitions.

The user has previously learned how to do something.

Later:

"How did I do this last time?"

Mael remembers the process.

Not just:

"Here's a generic tutorial."

But:

"Last time, we did this in three steps."

Then the steps appear.

1. Open Settings.
2. Select Security.
3. Choose Two-Factor Authentication.

What we're demonstrating:

Mael reduces the burden of having to remember every process.

## 08.6 — GUIDE

Now show an actual task.

Something like:

"Help me send this photo to my daughter."

Mael doesn't dump instructions.

He guides.

Step 1 of 3
Open the photo you'd like to send.

Then the user does it.
Mael waits.
Great. Now tap Share.
The important interaction is:
Mael doesn't rush ahead.
He stays with the person.
That's a huge part of the product philosophy.

## 08.7 — VERIFY

Now we introduce one of Mael's strongest use cases.

A suspicious message appears:

"Your bank account has been locked. Click here immediately to restore access."

The user asks:

"Is this real?"

The interface changes.
The Horizon appears subtly within the product.
Mael examines the message.
He identifies the warning signs.

And instead of simply saying:

SCAM 🚨

he explains:

"There are a few reasons I'd be cautious about this message."

Then:

The message creates urgency.
It asks you to click a link.
The sender doesn't match the official bank domain.
We can verify this another way.

Then:

"Let's check your bank's official website instead."

This is Mael's investigative identity appearing inside the product.

Not fear.

Verification.

## 08.8 — PROTECT

Then we show what happens when risk is actually present.
The suspicious link remains visible.

But Mael creates separation between:

the person
and
the risky action.

Something like:

Don't click the link yet.

Then:

Let's verify the message first.

The interface becomes calmer.
This is where the product communicates:

Mael isn't making decisions for you.
He's helping you make a safer one.

## 08.9 — The five behaviors become the product architecture

Now we can finally show them together:

Understand.

Make technology easier to comprehend.

Remember.

Keep important processes accessible.

Guide.

Move through tasks one step at a time.

Verify.

Investigate before you trust.

Protect.

Help prevent avoidable mistakes and scams.
And visually, these shouldn't necessarily be five equal cards.
I actually think we could arrange them as a journey around Mael's Horizon.

## 08.10 — The product should show the human, too

Every product demonstration should have a human context.

Not:

"User → AI → output."

Instead:

Person → uncertainty → Mael → understanding → person continues independently.

The person is always the protagonist.
Mael is the guide.
That is one of the most important product principles we've established.

## 08.11 — Accessibility

This section is also where we can introduce the practical things that matter to the target user.

Without turning it into a technical checklist.

For example:

Built for the way people actually use technology.

Then show:

- clear language
- voice interaction
- spoken explanations
- step-by-step guidance
- persistent context
- reduced cognitive load
- scam/verification assistance

And importantly:

These aren't "senior features."
They're good design.

## 08.12 — The caregiver/family side

Then we introduce the second audience.

Maybe:

And when you can't be there, Mael can be.
This needs careful product definition later, because we don't want to imply capabilities we haven't actually decided to build.
But visually, we can show the concept:

A daughter receives:

Mom asked Mael about a suspicious message.

or:

Mom successfully completed the account recovery process.
The daughter doesn't have to intervene.
That's the emotional payoff.

Not:

"Mael replaces you."

It's:

"You don't have to be on call for every little technology problem."

## 08.13 — Product demo

Then we give the visitor a slightly more traditional product demonstration.
Maybe a phone/laptop interface floating within the same dark environment.
But the cosmic aesthetic should now be subtle.
No giant galaxy behind the dashboard.

Instead:

dark background
restrained golden Horizon
clean interface
warm typography
lots of breathing room

The product itself should be extremely readable.
The cosmos belongs to Mael's identity.
Clarity belongs to Mael's product.

## 08.14 — The final product statement

After showing the experiences:

Technology doesn't need more complexity.

Then:

It needs someone who can help make sense of it.
And beneath:
That's Mael.
That brings us back to the character we met at the beginning.

